1
00:00:00,321 --> 00:00:02,971
On the data sheet, chlorine is element seventeen,

2
00:00:03,372 --> 00:00:05,841
so a chlorine atom has seventeen electrons.

3
00:00:06,584 --> 00:00:08,592
In Stage 5, you'd write them as two,

4
00:00:08,874 --> 00:00:09,616
eight, seven,

5
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,284
one number for each shell.

6
00:00:11,925 --> 00:00:13,665
But in the energy level video, we saw

7
00:00:13,706 --> 00:00:17,088
that inside each level, electrons occupy orbitals of

8
00:00:17,148 --> 00:00:17,788
different types,

9
00:00:18,409 --> 00:00:19,850
s, p, d and f.

10
00:00:20,614 --> 00:00:23,026
So where exactly is each of chlorine's seventeen

11
00:00:23,127 --> 00:00:23,589
electrons?

12
00:00:24,434 --> 00:00:26,402
Spdf notation answers that.

13
00:00:27,006 --> 00:00:28,812
It's like an address for every electron.

14
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:32,066
Start with the energy levels, numbered one, two,

15
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:34,671
three and four, going up in energy.

16
00:00:35,414 --> 00:00:37,148
Each level is split into subshells.

17
00:00:37,693 --> 00:00:39,397
A subshell is a group of orbitals of

18
00:00:39,438 --> 00:00:40,702
the same type and energy.

19
00:00:41,405 --> 00:00:43,353
Level one has just an s subshell.

20
00:00:43,855 --> 00:00:45,350
Level two has s and p.

21
00:00:45,836 --> 00:00:48,032
Level three has s, p and d.

22
00:00:48,577 --> 00:00:50,485
And level four has all four types,

23
00:00:51,008 --> 00:00:52,514
s, p, d and f.

24
00:00:53,158 --> 00:00:54,843
So the level number tells you how many

25
00:00:54,904 --> 00:00:56,107
subshell types it has.

26
00:00:57,061 --> 00:00:59,627
Each subshell is made of orbitals, the regions

27
00:00:59,668 --> 00:01:01,472
where an electron is likely to be found.

28
00:01:02,154 --> 00:01:04,577
An s subshell has one orbital, a p

29
00:01:04,598 --> 00:01:07,484
subshell has three, a d has five, and

30
00:01:07,504 --> 00:01:08,525
an f has seven.

31
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:09,860
Here's the key rule.

32
00:01:10,388 --> 00:01:13,440
Each orbital holds at most two electrons, and

33
00:01:13,481 --> 00:01:15,387
those two must spin in opposite directions.

34
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:18,060
We draw each orbital as a box, and

35
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:19,949
its electrons as an up arrow and a

36
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:20,572
down arrow.

37
00:01:21,095 --> 00:01:23,943
So an s subshell holds two electrons, p

38
00:01:24,005 --> 00:01:24,686
holds six,

39
00:01:25,209 --> 00:01:27,756
d holds ten, and f holds fourteen.

40
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:30,064
Now the notation itself.

41
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:32,095
Take three p five.

42
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,088
The three is the energy level.

43
00:01:34,512 --> 00:01:36,239
The p is the type of subshell.

44
00:01:36,802 --> 00:01:38,895
And the small raised five is the number

45
00:01:38,916 --> 00:01:39,921
of electrons in it.

46
00:01:40,608 --> 00:01:41,498
So it's an address.

47
00:01:42,227 --> 00:01:45,436
Level three, the p subshell, five electrons live

48
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:45,617
there.

49
00:01:46,539 --> 00:01:49,641
A full configuration lists every occupied subshell like

50
00:01:49,682 --> 00:01:49,881
this,

51
00:01:50,462 --> 00:01:52,422
and the raised numbers always add up to

52
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:54,244
the number of electrons in the atom.

53
00:01:55,216 --> 00:01:57,203
So which subshells fill first?

54
00:01:58,026 --> 00:02:01,110
Electrons fill the lowest energy subshells first, just

55
00:02:01,151 --> 00:02:03,014
as a ball settles on the lowest step

56
00:02:03,055 --> 00:02:03,735
of a staircase.

57
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:06,295
Mostly, that means level by level.

58
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:09,373
One s, two s, two p, three s,

59
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:10,258
three p.

60
00:02:10,781 --> 00:02:12,448
But there's a surprise at level four.

61
00:02:13,031 --> 00:02:15,413
The four s subshell is lower in energy

62
00:02:15,455 --> 00:02:16,275
than three d,

63
00:02:16,777 --> 00:02:19,320
so four s fills first, and three d

64
00:02:19,381 --> 00:02:20,201
fills after it.

65
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:21,606
Quick prediction.

66
00:02:22,215 --> 00:02:24,074
Potassium has nineteen electrons.

67
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:26,969
Once three p is full, the atom holds

68
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:27,592
eighteen.

69
00:02:28,156 --> 00:02:29,623
Where does electron nineteen go?

70
00:02:33,409 --> 00:02:35,524
Into four s, not three d.

71
00:02:36,068 --> 00:02:38,473
Four s is the lower energy subshell, so

72
00:02:38,674 --> 00:02:41,259
it fills first, and potassium ends in four

73
00:02:41,481 --> 00:02:41,921
s one.

74
00:02:42,544 --> 00:02:44,575
That's also why potassium starts a new row

75
00:02:44,616 --> 00:02:45,500
of the periodic table.

76
00:02:46,084 --> 00:02:48,272
Its outermost electron is the first one in

77
00:02:48,313 --> 00:02:48,774
level four.

78
00:02:49,499 --> 00:02:52,153
Calcium, with twenty electrons, fills four s with

79
00:02:52,194 --> 00:02:52,816
a second one.

80
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:56,155
Only then, from scandium on, does three d

81
00:02:56,196 --> 00:02:56,798
start to fill.

82
00:02:57,421 --> 00:02:59,889
Let's build phosphorus, with fifteen electrons.

83
00:03:00,593 --> 00:03:02,168
One s two, that's two.

84
00:03:02,653 --> 00:03:03,319
Two s two,

85
00:03:03,865 --> 00:03:04,127
four.

86
00:03:04,632 --> 00:03:05,867
Two p six, ten.

87
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:07,118
Three s two,

88
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:07,943
twelve.

89
00:03:08,365 --> 00:03:10,812
And three p takes the last three, making

90
00:03:10,953 --> 00:03:11,394
fifteen.

91
00:03:12,056 --> 00:03:14,620
So phosphorus is one s two, two s

92
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,446
two, two p six, three s two, three

93
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:17,967
p three.

94
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:20,638
Group the levels, and you get two, eight,

95
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,627
five, the Stage 5 version.

96
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:25,438
But how do those three p electrons sit

97
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:26,745
in their three orbitals?

98
00:03:27,591 --> 00:03:28,680
Electrons repel each other.

99
00:03:29,163 --> 00:03:31,407
So in orbitals of equal energy, they spread

100
00:03:31,509 --> 00:03:31,628
out,

101
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:34,214
one per orbital, before any of them pair

102
00:03:34,295 --> 00:03:34,375
up.

103
00:03:35,018 --> 00:03:37,525
Phosphorus's three p electrons each get their own

104
00:03:37,586 --> 00:03:37,806
box.

105
00:03:38,629 --> 00:03:42,380
That gives phosphorus three unpaired electrons, electrons alone

106
00:03:42,461 --> 00:03:43,022
in their orbital.

107
00:03:43,775 --> 00:03:46,881
Chlorine has seventeen, so its three p subshell

108
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:47,683
holds five.

109
00:03:48,386 --> 00:03:50,637
The first three go one per box, and

110
00:03:50,658 --> 00:03:51,723
the next two pair up.

111
00:03:52,327 --> 00:03:54,975
That leaves two pairs and one single, so

112
00:03:54,996 --> 00:03:57,403
chlorine has one unpaired electron.

113
00:03:58,228 --> 00:03:59,113
We can shorten it, too.

114
00:03:59,858 --> 00:04:01,862
Everything up to two p six matches the

115
00:04:01,963 --> 00:04:04,689
noble gas neon, so we write neon in

116
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:07,416
square brackets, then three s two, three p

117
00:04:07,457 --> 00:04:07,677
five.

118
00:04:08,651 --> 00:04:10,221
Now iron, with twenty-six.

119
00:04:11,188 --> 00:04:13,739
After three p six, eighteen electrons are placed,

120
00:04:14,021 --> 00:04:14,964
the same as argon.

121
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:18,345
Four s fills next with two, making twenty.

122
00:04:18,929 --> 00:04:20,616
The last six go into three d.

123
00:04:21,379 --> 00:04:23,342
When we write it, we list the subshells

124
00:04:23,443 --> 00:04:26,007
in level order, so three d comes before

125
00:04:26,128 --> 00:04:26,568
four s.

126
00:04:27,332 --> 00:04:29,586
Argon, three d six, four s two.

127
00:04:30,392 --> 00:04:32,795
In the five d boxes, six electrons means

128
00:04:32,997 --> 00:04:35,060
five singles, then one pair.

129
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:38,109
So iron has four unpaired electrons.

130
00:04:39,304 --> 00:04:39,868
One warning.

131
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:42,499
A configuration can add up and still be

132
00:04:42,539 --> 00:04:42,780
wrong.

133
00:04:43,723 --> 00:04:45,550
One s two, two s two, two p

134
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:48,280
five, three s two, three p six also

135
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:49,283
adds to seventeen.

136
00:04:49,805 --> 00:04:51,232
But two p isn't full.

137
00:04:52,035 --> 00:04:54,220
An electron won't go into a higher subshell

138
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:56,003
while a lower one still has room,

139
00:04:56,504 --> 00:04:58,588
so this atom isn't in its lowest energy

140
00:04:58,648 --> 00:04:59,049
arrangement.

141
00:04:59,653 --> 00:05:01,507
Check the order, as well as the total.

142
00:05:02,137 --> 00:05:04,175
So here's the method in one line.

143
00:05:04,878 --> 00:05:07,980
Lowest first, one per box, then pair, and

144
00:05:08,021 --> 00:05:08,561
check the total.

145
00:05:09,382 --> 00:05:12,485
Fill from the lowest energy, remembering four s

146
00:05:12,566 --> 00:05:13,385
before three d.

147
00:05:14,309 --> 00:05:15,475
Spread out before pairing.

148
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:18,287
And make sure the raised numbers add up

149
00:05:18,449 --> 00:05:19,372
to the electron count.

150
00:05:22,057 --> 00:05:22,480
Your turn.

151
00:05:23,166 --> 00:05:25,357
Sulfur has atomic number sixteen.

152
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:28,502
Write the full electron configuration of a sulfur

153
00:05:28,623 --> 00:05:31,606
atom, using s p d f notation, and

154
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:34,029
state how many unpaired electrons it has.

155
00:05:34,773 --> 00:05:35,949
Pause here to answer.

156
00:05:46,527 --> 00:05:48,968
Sulfur is one s two, two s two,

157
00:05:49,269 --> 00:05:51,970
two p six, three s two, three p

158
00:05:52,091 --> 00:05:53,491
four, adding to sixteen.

159
00:05:54,334 --> 00:05:56,379
In three p, the first three go one

160
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:58,385
per box, and the fourth pairs up.

161
00:05:58,948 --> 00:06:01,915
That's one pair and two singles, so two

162
00:06:02,117 --> 00:06:03,058
unpaired electrons.

163
00:06:03,741 --> 00:06:05,994
Pairing them up first, to get zero unpaired,

164
00:06:06,438 --> 00:06:07,303
is the classic slip.

165
00:06:07,949 --> 00:06:09,342
One per box comes first.

166
00:06:11,037 --> 00:06:11,421
Last one.

167
00:06:12,187 --> 00:06:14,837
Vanadium has atomic number twenty-three.

168
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:18,908
Write its electron configuration using noble gas shorthand,

169
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:22,418
and state how many unpaired electrons a vanadium

170
00:06:22,539 --> 00:06:23,099
atom has.

171
00:06:23,722 --> 00:06:24,490
Pause here to answer.

172
00:06:35,236 --> 00:06:36,320
Argon takes eighteen.

173
00:06:36,985 --> 00:06:39,675
Four s fills next with two, making twenty,

174
00:06:40,098 --> 00:06:41,984
and the last three go into three d.

175
00:06:42,608 --> 00:06:45,983
So vanadium is argon, three d three, four

176
00:06:46,105 --> 00:06:46,425
s two.

177
00:06:47,068 --> 00:06:49,272
The three d electrons go one per box,

178
00:06:49,593 --> 00:06:51,597
so there are three unpaired electrons.

179
00:06:52,319 --> 00:06:55,367
Writing argon, three d five, is the classic

180
00:06:55,388 --> 00:06:55,608
slip.

181
00:06:56,090 --> 00:06:58,055
It fills three d before four s, and

182
00:06:58,197 --> 00:06:59,119
four s comes first.

183
00:07:01,085 --> 00:07:02,437
Now look where our atoms sit.

184
00:07:03,203 --> 00:07:05,736
Phosphorus and chlorine both end in three p,

185
00:07:05,957 --> 00:07:06,761
and they share a row.

186
00:07:07,284 --> 00:07:10,011
Potassium's four s one begins the next row,

187
00:07:10,392 --> 00:07:12,438
and iron is filling three d, in the

188
00:07:12,458 --> 00:07:12,939
middle block.

189
00:07:13,502 --> 00:07:14,266
That's no accident.

190
00:07:15,131 --> 00:07:18,128
Next, how do electron arrangements organise groups and

191
00:07:18,228 --> 00:07:18,711
periods?

192
00:07:21,461 --> 00:07:22,284
Here are the key notes.

193
00:07:22,807 --> 00:07:24,355
Pause the video if you need more time

194
00:07:24,395 --> 00:07:24,817
to copy them.
